Troubleshooting
Issue: backup-database.sh fails with a permission denied error when writing to /backup/satellite/database/. Diagnostic hint: Check the ownership and mode of the backup directory with ls -ld /backup/satellite/database and confirm the script is being run with sufficient privileges (root or a user in the postgres group) to both read the database and write to that path.

Issue: test-db-restore.sh reports zero tables restored even though the script exits without an error. Diagnostic hint: Inspect /tmp/test_restore_output.log for psql errors that may have been suppressed by ON_ERROR_STOP=0; a dump created against a different PostgreSQL major version or a truncated .sql.gz file are common causes.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete, working backup and disaster recovery workflow for Red Hat Satellite 6, covering database dumps, configuration archiving, scheduled execution via cron, and documented recovery procedures. You then proved the value of that workflow by restoring both the database and configuration backups into isolated test locations, confirming that the backups were not just present but actually usable, and captured the results in a repeatable test report. Along the way you:

Created robust backup scripts for the Satellite database and configuration files, with logging, compression, and retention cleanup built in
Automated backup scheduling using cron so backups run consistently without manual intervention
Documented a phased disaster recovery plan with clear recovery time and recovery point objectives
Built working restore scripts for both the database and configuration data
Tested backup and recovery processes in a controlled environment to validate their effectiveness
Documented comprehensive disaster recovery procedures for operational use
This comprehensive disaster recovery plan ensures that Red Hat Satellite 6 can be restored quickly and reliably in the event of a disaster, minimizing downtime and data loss.
